  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday through Sunday)\nIssued at 205 PM CDT Mon Sep 14 2026\n\nMidweek, an upper level ridge shall overspread much of the eastern \nhalf of the CONUS, centered near the southeastern coast. Our region \nlooks to lie along its western periphery.The core of the ridge \nbegins to shift west Wednesday night into Thursday. However, it \nbecomes compressed by an upper trough near the Pacific Northwest and \nan inverted trough rounding its southern edge in the Gulf. These \nwork together to gradually erode the ridge, continuing lower heights\nand relatively cooler temperatures. Highs through the remainder of\nthe work week are forecast to reach the 90s at most locations. Triple\ndigit heat becomes restricted to isolated spots along the Rio \nGrande, while highs in the 80s will be common within the higher \nterrain and adjacent areas. Upslope flow combined with passing \ndisturbances promote isolated to scattered shower/storm \ndevelopment over the western half of our region through the rest \nof the week. The best odds for rainfall will tend to favor the \nhigher terrain. \n\nThis weekend, the potential for rainfall becomes more widespread,\naccompanied by even cooler temperatures. These come courtesy of \nthe aforementioned trough moving in from the west, providing a \nbetter source of lift and moisture, and, eventually, a cold front.\nAlthough, it's important to note that there remains uncertainty \nregarding details and specific impacts with this trough. For now, \nhighs in the 80s gradually envelop the region from northwest to \nsoutheast through the weekend and into early next week. Otherwise,\nexpect highs in the low to mid 90s. Overnight lows through the \nLong Term period will generally range in the 60s/70s, with 60s \nbeing more prevalent toward the end of the period. As per the \nlatest data, isolated to scattered showers/storms will be possible\nareawide through the weekend. We'll have to see how the story \nunfolds going forward, so stay posted!",
